<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">István Széchenyi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Count István Széchenyi was a Hungarian reformer and statesman, called 'the greatest Hungarian' by his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth">Lajos Kossuth</a>. He funded the founding of the Hungarian Academy of Sciences in 1825, promoted steam navigation on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the regulation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>, and initiated the Chain Bridge between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> and Pest. His books urged the nobility to give up its privileges, and he served in the government of 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/istvan-szechenyi.jpg" alt="István Széchenyi" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miklós Barabás · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gr%C3%B3f_Sz%C3%A9chenyi_Istv%C3%A1n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>István Széchenyi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> to Count Ferenc Széchényi and Countess Juliána Festetics de Tolna. The Széchenyis were an old and influential Hungarian noble family, traditionally loyal to the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>. His father, an enlightened aristocrat, founded the Hungarian National Museum and the Hungarian National Library.</p><p>At seventeen he joined the Austrian army and fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, distinguishing himself at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raba">Raab</a> on 14 June 1809 and carrying messages through enemy lines before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a> in October 1813. He left the service as a captain in 1826, after a promotion to major was turned down, and from September 1815 to 1821 he had travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. The rapid modernisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> fascinated him most.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Széchenyi organised the National Casino in 1827 as a forum for the patriotic nobility. His political writings, Hitel (Credit, 1830), Világ (Light, 1831) and Stádium (1833), condemned the nobility's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a> and urged it to give up feudal privileges such as freedom from taxation and to lead modernisation. He envisioned reform within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, opposed radicalism, and thought nationalism particularly dangerous in the multi-ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>. In 1835 he established the Óbuda Shipyard, the first industrial-scale steamship builder in the Habsburg Empire. He led the Danube Navigation Committee, which finished its work in ten years, and was the first to promote steamboats on the Danube, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-balaton">Lake Balaton</a>.</p><p>He backed the Chain Bridge, the first permanent bridge between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> and Pest, as a symbol of their later union. He disagreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth">Lajos Kossuth</a>, whom he thought an agitator: in his pamphlet People of the East (1841) he argued for slow, careful reform to avoid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> intervention, favouring laissez-faire and agriculture where Kossuth favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protective-tariff">protective tariffs</a> and rapid industrialisation. In March 1848 he accepted the portfolio of ways and communications under Lajos Batthyány.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As the revolution of 1848 moved towards an attempt to break from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule, contrary to his vision, Széchenyi suffered a breakdown in early September and was placed in Dr Gustav Görgen's asylum at Oberdöbling. He recovered enough to write Önismeret and Ein Blick, but never returned to politics. Still depressed, he shot himself on 8 April 1860, aged 68. All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> mourned, and more than 50,000 people visited his grave at Nagycenk in the three days after his funeral. A statue was unveiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> on 23 May 1880, and in 1898 the Chain Bridge was named the Széchenyi Chain Bridge in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “István Széchenyi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Revolts by Bocskai, Thököly and Rákóczi defended Protestant and noble rights; the Reform Era brought <b>Széchenyi</b>'s modernisation.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hungary-1526-1867">Kingdom of Hungary (1526-1867)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Döbling, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1835" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of István Széchenyi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1835">What was here in 1835? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
